#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
/usr/local/sbin/nordvpn-select

Pick a live, lightly-loaded NordVPN WireGuard server in a given US state and
move the tunnel onto it WITHOUT dropping the interface.

WHY THIS EXISTS
---------------
The old design stored a pinned server per state, in
~/.local/share/nordvpn-states/<state>.yaml. Every stored endpoint is an outage
with a timer on it: NordVPN retires servers on their own schedule and tells
nobody. colorado.yaml pinned 212.102.45.117, which was withdrawn, so that menu
entry failed while georgia.yaml kept working purely by luck of the draw.
Storing a server is the bug. This resolves one at switch time instead.

WHY `wg set` AND NOT nmcli down/up
----------------------------------
The connection's routing is:

    31000: from all lookup main suppress_prefixlength 0
    31001: not from all fwmark 0xcc57 lookup 52311
    table 52311: default dev nordvpn
    main:        default via 192.168.12.1 dev enxf8b46a8d5cdf

While the tunnel is up there is no way out except through it. But rules 31000
and 31001 and table 52311 are owned by NetworkManager and are torn down the
instant the connection goes down - at which point main's default route via the
physical NIC is uncovered and every packet leaves in clear text. So `nmcli
connection down` is not a brief drop, it is a brief total bypass. The old tray
did exactly that on every state switch.

WireGuard does not need a teardown to change peers. Swapping the peer on the
live interface means the device never leaves, rules 31000/31001 are never torn
down, and packets sent mid-swap hit the wg device and are dropped because no
handshake exists yet. That is fail-closed by construction: the protection is
the absence of a fallback route, not a rule someone has to remember to install.

The private key is never read or written here. Only the peer changes.

WHY NETPLAN IS WRITTEN BUT NOT APPLIED
--------------------------------------
`netplan apply` cycles the connection, which is the leak this avoids. The live
swap is what takes effect now; the netplan write is only so a reboot comes back
to the same server. Persistence is delegated to /usr/local/bin/nordvpn-repin,
which owns the three-coordinated-edits problem (endpoint, keys.public, and the
passthrough key literally NAMED wireguard-peer.<PUBKEY>.persistent-keepalive).
Keeping one implementation of that is deliberate - an earlier sed version
silently matched nothing against the real file.

WHY THE STATE TABLE IS LOCAL
----------------------------
NordVPN has no concept of a state. Its geography is country -> city and the
only coarser grouping is "The Americas". The table below was built by joining
their US city list (55 cities) against state names; the join was exact in both
directions and covers all 50 states. Only California, Missouri, New York and
Texas have more than one city, so for most states this resolves to a single
city and the real selection happens between servers inside it.

Usage:
  nordvpn-select --list                 print known states
  nordvpn-select --status               current peer, its load, best available
  nordvpn-select --state Colorado       switch to the best server in that state
  nordvpn-select --poll                 hourly check: heal if dead, move only
                                        if something is MARGIN points better

Exit codes: 0 ok/changed, 1 nothing to do, 2 error.  Requires root.
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request

